      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2024551557732192530",
      "url": "https://x.com/HumanityChad/status/2024551557732192530",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/HumanityChad/status/2024551557732192530",
      "text": "Rare Footage of a Gunner Laid to Rest With His Aircraft 🫡\n\nIn 1942, Loyce Edward Deen left his home in Oklahoma to join the U.S. Navy. Serving as an aerial gunner aboard a TBF Avenger torpedo bomber on the aircraft carrier USS Essex, he took part in numerous Pacific operations, including the Philippines, Iwo Jima, Leyte, and others.\n\nOn November 5, 1944, during a mission over Manila Bay, his aircraft was struck by enemy anti-aircraft fire. Loyce, just 23 years old, was killed instantly. Footage shows sailors taking his fingerprints and retrieving his dog tag - yet they never remove him from his seat.\n\nWhy? The answer is both simple and profoundly moving. The aircraft, too severely damaged to be repaired, was scheduled to be committed to the sea.\n\nIn an extraordinarily rare tribute for the time, especially during an era when every airplane was precious, Loyce Deen was laid to rest still strapped in the cockpit of the plane he flew. The crew gathered on deck to bid him farewell, honoring both the man and the machine that carried him through battle.",
